.git
node_modules
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
user-interviews
adr
docs
# Published open data, not an image input: the Dockerfile copies named paths and never this one,
# so keeping it out of the build context spares the daemon a transfer nothing in the image uses.
exports

# Environment files never enter the build context, so no layer — and no layer cache — can carry a
# secret even if a Dockerfile line were added that copied one. This is the second half of the rule
# the Dockerfile states: configuration reaches the container through the ECS task definition the
# deploy job assembles from Secrets Manager, never through the image. `scripts/check-deploy.mjs`
# fails CI if either half is removed.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# The deploy job's two plaintext scratch files (packages/api/docs/deploy.md §2). They are written
# under $RUNNER_TEMP, not here — these patterns exist so that a run which writes one into the
# checkout root, or a developer who reproduces the deploy locally, still cannot hand it to the
# daemon as build context. Same rule as the env patterns above, same reason: a `mode=max` layer
# cache in a PUBLIC repository. `scripts/check-deploy.mjs` fails CI if either is removed.
task-definition*.json
container-env*.json

# The frontend is not an input to the API image. It is deployed on its own (see its README) and
# the API image must not be able to fail — and so block an ECS deploy — on a package the service
# never runs, which `pnpm -r build` / `pnpm -r typecheck` in the builder stage would otherwise do.
#
# NOTE for whoever adds packages/frontend: the builder runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, and
# a workspace member present in pnpm-lock.yaml but missing from the context makes that command
# refuse the lockfile. Excluding the directory therefore has to come with keeping the frontend out
# of the API's lockfile importers (its own lockfile / not a `packages/*` workspace member), or with
# copying it in and building only the API package. Validate with a local `docker build` at that point.
packages/frontend

# packages/e2e is the same story as packages/frontend above, for the same reason: it is not an
# input to the API image (it orchestrates the API from OUTSIDE the container — Docker, a disposable
# Postgres, a browser — it is not something that runs inside one), and the builder's
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` has to keep tolerating a workspace member present in
# pnpm-lock.yaml but absent from the build context. Validated by the same local `docker build` step.
packages/e2e
